## Runtime Configuration

Copy `.env.example` to root `.env` for Compose. Resolution is process environment > selected local configuration file > nonsecret defaults.
The backend resolves the default dotenv file from source location, so backend-CWD startup also reads root `.env`.
To select another file, set `LIFTOFF_ENV_FILE` to its path relative to the startup working directory; this backend expects dotenv.
An explicitly selected missing/unreadable file fails; configuration is never shell-sourced.
Malformed dotenv assignments or unbalanced quotes fail rather than being silently ignored. Use `KEY=value`, optional `export`, comments, and single/double-quoted values.
Node uses the runtime dotenv parser; escaped quote delimiters are rejected to avoid truncation. Use the other quote style when a value contains a quote.

Local native URLs use localhost. Start supporting services and then the native backend:

```bash
cp .env.example .env
docker compose up -d postgres redis azurite mailpit
(cd backend && npm run build && npm start)
```


Compose reads root `.env` for interpolation; select another file with `docker compose --env-file environments/dev/backend.env up --build`.
Compose deliberately replaces database, Redis, and blob service addresses with container-reachable names while forwarding applicable model, transport, and tracing inputs.
`/health` and `/ready` are local process/configuration endpoints, not proof of external-service connectivity or production readiness.

The backend requires `DATABASE_URL` and `REDIS_URL`. `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` is a comma-separated allowlist and defaults to the local frontend at `http://localhost:5173`. No GenAI model or tracing credentials are required.

